{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And these you shall detest among the birds</b> — It is not from all birds that they are forbidden, but only those mentioned in this passage alone, together with their species, as those regarding which it says \"לְמִינוֹ\" and \"לְמִינָהּ\" (after its kind / after her kind). For Scripture did not mention a sign in an impure bird for prohibition or in a pure one for permissibility, but said \"And these you shall detest among the birds\" — not others besides these. And likewise the creeping creatures that impart impurity, which it specified individually (below, verses 29–30), are not subject to this rule except those named by name in the passage. And our Sages learned signs for birds (Ḥullin 59a), and they are to recognize by them that any bird possessing those signs is not one of these forbidden species. And the great sign among birds is the derisah (grasping prey), for any bird that performs derisah is invariably impure, for the Torah distanced it because its blood is heated by its cruelty and is black and coarse, and it produces burnt black gall, and instills cruelty in the heart. And there is no bird in all the world that performs derisah besides those mentioned in the passage. Now, any bird that performs derisah — we know that it is one of those mentioned, and if it is known that it does not perform derisah, it is certainly permitted, for there is among all the forbidden birds no bird that does not perform derisah except one, namely the peres or the ozniah. And the Sages were not concerned about it because it is not found in settled lands (Ḥullin 62a), but dwells continually in the wildernesses. And perhaps because it dwells in desolate wastes its blood is burnt and evil like those that perform derisah, and the Torah forbade it together with them. And the Sages further enumerated signs: if a bird is found that has an extra toe and a zapek (crop) and a peelable qurqeban (gizzard), it is certainly pure, for it is known to them that it does not perform derisah. But if it has two of the three signs mentioned, it is forbidden, for the raven has an extra toe and a peelable qurqeban, and we are suspicious of it and of its species, for they all possess two signs. And it goes without saying that if it had only one of the three signs mentioned, it would be forbidden, for the rest of the forbidden birds are all thus with one sign, except the nesher (eagle/vulture), which has none of these three at all. And the meaning of derisah is catching prey — that it pursues birds and seizes them alive and grasps them in its talons and eats them, as the large hawk called ostur and the small hawk called esploir in Old French do, and in Arabic, bīṭ. And this is the law of birds in their prohibition and their permissibility by the correct and established way in the Gemara, and which we have investigated and found to be so through examination of birds. Now, the reason for the prohibition of birds is because of the cruelty of their natures. And the beasts — it is possible that it is likewise because among those with split hooves and chewing the cud there is none that performs derisah, and all the others tear prey. And we find in their natures a difference from what the Sages mentioned (Avodah Zarah 35a), that all the fat of pure animals congeals, while the fat of all impure animals never coagulates and never hardens at all — and here they are different. And from this it is possible that they cause harm to the organs of generation, and the seed that accumulates from the moisture in them will be cold and damp, and will not produce offspring at all, or will not produce well and properly. Besides that there is in the permitted ones a known benefit in the way of medicine. And I have seen in certain books of empirical remedies that if a nursing infant suckles from the fat of the pig, that child will become a leper. And this is a sign that there are in all of them very evil properties."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The Old French glosses אסטו\"ר and אשפרוי\"ר are rendered as ostur (ostor / goshawk) and esploir (sparrowhawk) respectively, following standard medieval ornithological identifications in rabbinic commentaries."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:04.769944477Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}